{
  "pages": [
    {
      "page_idx": 18,
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "header",
          "bbox": [
            0.262,
            0.093,
            0.76,
            0.121
          ],
          "content": "Q U E S T I O N S"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.124,
            0.139,
            0.77,
            0.172
          ],
          "content": "**1. Metallic oxides of zinc, magnesium and copper were heated with the following metals.**"
        },
        {
          "type": "table",
          "bbox": [
            0.174,
            0.178,
            0.678,
            0.277
          ],
          "content": "| Metal | Zinc | Magnesium | Copper |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Zinc oxideMagnesium oxideCopper oxide |  |  |  |",
          "captions": [],
          "footnotes": [],
          "image_source": "images/page_18_table_2.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.171,
            0.287,
            0.739,
            0.306
          ],
          "content": "**In which cases will you find displacement reactions taking place?**"
        },
        {
          "type": "footer",
          "bbox": [
            0.448,
            0.971,
            0.552,
            0.985
          ],
          "content": "Reprint 2026-27"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.124,
            0.309,
            0.491,
            0.327
          ],
          "content": "**2. Which metals do not corrode easily?**"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.123,
            0.331,
            0.324,
            0.35
          ],
          "content": "**3. What are alloys?**"
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "bbox": [
            0.159,
            0.391,
            0.54,
            0.419
          ],
          "level": 2,
          "content": "What you have learnt"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.139,
            0.436,
            0.654,
            0.453
          ],
          "content": "n **Elements can be classified as metals and non-metals.**"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.139,
            0.456,
            0.891,
            0.491
          ],
          "content": "Metals are lustrous, malleable, ductile and are good conductors of heat and **electricity. They are solids at room temperature, except mercury which is a liquid.**"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.138,
            0.494,
            0.743,
            0.511
          ],
          "content": "Metals can form positive ions by losing electrons to non-metals."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.139,
            0.514,
            0.892,
            0.565
          ],
          "content": "Metals combine with oxygen to form basic oxides. Aluminium oxide and zinc oxide **show the properties of both basic as well as acidic oxides. These oxides are known as amphoteric oxides.**"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.138,
            0.568,
            0.794,
            0.586
          ],
          "content": "n **Different metals have different reactivities with water and dilute acids.**"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.138,
            0.589,
            0.891,
            0.623
          ],
          "content": "A list of common metals arranged in order of their decreasing reactivity is known **as an activity series.**"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.139,
            0.626,
            0.891,
            0.66
          ],
          "content": "n **Metals above hydrogen in the Activity series can displace hydrogen from dilute acids.**"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.138,
            0.663,
            0.833,
            0.681
          ],
          "content": "A more reactive metal displaces a less reactive metal from its salt solution."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.139,
            0.684,
            0.836,
            0.701
          ],
          "content": "n **Metals occur in nature as free elements or in the form of their compounds.**"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.139,
            0.704,
            0.892,
            0.739
          ],
          "content": "The extraction of metals from their ores and then refining them for use is known **as metallurgy.**"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.139,
            0.742,
            0.892,
            0.775
          ],
          "content": "n **An alloy is a homogeneous mixture of two or more metals, or a metal and a non-metal.**"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.139,
            0.779,
            0.891,
            0.813
          ],
          "content": "The surface of some metals, such as iron, is corroded when they are exposed to **moist air for a long period of time. This phenomenon is known as corrosion.**"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.138,
            0.816,
            0.891,
            0.867
          ],
          "content": "Non-metals have properties opposite to that of metals. They are neither malleable **nor ductile. They are bad conductors of heat and electricity, except for graphite, which conducts electricity.**"
        },
        {
          "type": "footer",
          "bbox": [
            0.1,
            0.916,
            0.269,
            0.93
          ],
          "content": "Metals and Non-metals"
        },
        {
          "type": "page_number",
          "bbox": [
            0.884,
